{:frame
 {:question "Is the K16 a murakumo node?"
  :answer-before "No, and the shape of the gap was invisible: the Mac relay was
                  complete -- enrollment, claim, dispatch, result verification,
                  heartbeat, an expectation table -- and the BOARD implemented
                  none of it. `grep -l micro-infer os/aiueos/native/*.kotoba`
                  matched nothing. Every 'murakumo node' claim so far was a
                  relay talking to itself."
  :answer-now "The board computes and returns real answers. The relay hears it,
               accepts it, and stops at a credential this session must not go
               looking for."}

 :hypotheses
 [{:id "infer-h1"
   :claim "The frozen char bigram can live in the native word-typed subset."
   :state :confirmed
   :design "27 rows x 27 counts, every count <= 5, so a row packs losslessly
            into two i64 literals of 4-bit fields -- 15 cells in `lo` (bits
            0..59, clear of the sign bit) and 12 in `hi`. Generated from
            kernel/micro_infer.c, which stays the model's source of truth.
            The argmax is NOT precomputed: precomputing it would make the
            board a lookup table and the claim being qualified is that the
            K16 performed the reduction."
   :measured "Executed on the JS backend, prompt \"murakum\" returns token 111
              = 'o', score 2, total 5 -- the contract's :known-answer, arrived
              at by RUNNING the module. All 20 non-empty rows equal
              MURAKUMO_MICRO_INFER_ROWS, 0 mismatches."
   :defect-found-in-itself "The first generator packed with Math.pow and lost
              the top cells silently past 2^53 (16^14 = 7.2e16). Its own
              round-trip assertion caught it before anything was written."}

  {:id "infer-h2"
   :claim "The compile-time KIR oracle can be the gate: it folds an effect-free
           i64 main, so (quot 1 (- 1 (self-check))) compiles iff every row
           reduces to its expected value."
   :state :confirmed
   :measured "intact -> {:ok true}; row 13's expectation mutated ->
              :kotoba/lowering-failed \"division-by-zero\". Both directions
              seen, and the gate carries the control every run."
   :defect-found-in-itself "The control reported \"this gate cannot go red\"
              whenever the base table was ALREADY red, because two drifted
              rows make (quot 1 (- 1 2)) a legal -1. It now SKIPS and says so:
              MICRO_INFER_CONTROL skipped reason=base-table-already-red.
              A control that means nothing must not print like one that
              passed -- the 4th question of the eight."}

  {:id "relay-h1"
   :claim "The relay can ride bus3, and the contract's bus2 transport never
           could."
   :state :confirmed
   :measured "bus2 has no UDP receive path: the TCP stack owns that ring and
              re-initialises it every cycle. No job could ever have arrived on
              the wire the contract named, and nothing said so because nothing
              had tried. bus3's ring is installed once at debug-init and
              polled every cycle; four jobs have now crossed it."}

  {:id "relay-h2"
   :claim "The board's answers are computed, not echoed."
   :state :confirmed
   :measured "Four prompts chosen by the Mac, four different answers, four
              different job ids echoed, four different boot nonces, four
              different TSC cycle counts: murakum -> o/2/5/1152,
              aiueos -> ' '/5/7/1344, kotoba -> i/2/8/800, wave -> ' '/2/9/864.
              A constant would have failed three of four."
   :negative-control "173 datagrams carrying prompts the board must refuse --
              \"z\" (all-zero transition row) and \"aQ\" (outside the
              vocabulary) -- produced ZERO result lines. The bus2 netlog names
              which refusal each was: D9 73 x3 (empty row), D9 72 x3 (outside
              \" a-z\")."
   :independent-instrument "The answers crossed LAN2; the receipts are on the
              bus2 netlog -- a different NIC, a different socket. It counted
              D8 00 on 21 consecutive boots and D9 70 exactly four times, one
              per verified answer. Two instruments, one story."}

  {:id "relay-h3"
   :claim "The relay would have rejected the board's own announcement."
   :state :confirmed
   :measured "MURAKUMO_EXPECTED_MAC was one value, bus2's ...b6:32. The
              announcement leaves by the NIC it is sent from, bus3's ...b6:31.
              Same shape as the PXE server's MAC bug two days earlier, fixed
              the same way. The selftest was watched failing in both
              directions: drop b6:31 -> the bus3 hello asserts; add
              aa-bb-cc-dd-ee-ff -> the foreign hello asserts."}

  {:id "relay-h4"
   :claim "A one-byte command must be a datagram whose UDP length says one
           byte."
   :state :confirmed
   :measured "Once the relay owned port 9000 and began acknowledging, the board
              answered '?' to the Mac's own 55-byte AIUEOS_NODE_ACK_V1 -- its
              first payload byte is 'A', which is neither P nor R. The NIC pads
              short frames to 60, so the descriptor's byte count cannot
              discriminate; the UDP length field can. 23 boots / 22 spurious
              '?' before, 4 boots / 0 after."}

  {:id "budget-h1"
   :claim "Liveness ping/pong fits."
   :state :refuted
   :measured ":kotoba.error/subset-reject -- \"lowered program budget
              exhausted\". The native frontend has a hard 100,000-node
              lowered-program budget for the WHOLE program (max-lowered-nodes
              in kotoba-sema; not a flag). Moving the bigram self-check into
              its own module -- which the kernel deliberately does not require
              -- bought room and was not enough."
   :consequence "The K16 kernel is at the language's ceiling. The next feature
              on this board has to BUY its space rather than add to it. The
              pong's templates, offsets and relay/decimal-value stay in place;
              the handler is withdrawn rather than shipped half-linked."}]

